#
# sources - Windows 2000 DDK build description for xhci98.sys.
#
# Build from a DDK environment prompt (scripts\local\ddk-debug.cmd), then
# `cd src && build`. Debug output: src\objchk\i386\xhci98.sys; release
# output: src\objfre\i386\xhci98.sys.
#
# Prerequisite: usbport.lib must exist in this directory. It is a generated
# import library, not a checked-in one (*.lib is git-ignored) - run
# scripts\make-usbport-lib.cmd after a fresh clone.
#

TARGETNAME=xhci98
TARGETTYPE=DRIVER
TARGETPATH=obj

INCLUDES=$(BASEDIR)\inc;$(BASEDIR)\inc\ddk

#
# xhci_mem.c, xhci_ring.c, xhci_caps.c, xhci_port.c, xhci_ctx.c and xhci_xfer.c
# are the pure hardware-logic core: no DDK dependency, no MMIO, so
# test\run-host-tests.cmd compiles the same files on the build host
# (docs\contributing\design\03-host-unit-tests.md). Keep it that way - a file that needs
# ntddk.h to compile does not belong in that list.
#
# xhci_ctx.c is the Phase 6 Slot/Endpoint/Input Control Context encoder. It
# writes into caller-supplied common-buffer memory exactly as xhci_ring.c does,
# which is what keeps the golden vectors for every speed and both context
# strides a host test rather than a VM run.
#
# xhci_xfer.c is the Phase 6 control-transfer engine, and it is the first pure
# file that also includes xhci_usbport.h - the miniport ABI declarations, which
# are themselves DDK-free (test\test_packet.c compiles them here). It speaks the
# transfer ABI; it still touches no register and calls no usbport service.
#
# xhci_desc.c is task 9-A.2's configuration-descriptor snoop, on exactly the
# terms below: it is fed reply bytes the caller has already copied out of a
# mapped transfer buffer, keeps no pointer usbport owns, and decides nothing -
# the isochronous `bInterval` it recovers is converted into an Endpoint Context
# Interval by xhci_ctx.c and applied by the endpoint open.
#
# xhci_topo.c is the Phase 7b hub topology graph on the same terms: it reads
# snooped hub-class setup packets and reply bytes the *caller* has already
# copied out, mutates only the XHCI_TOPOLOGY it is handed, and never
# dereferences a pointer usbport owns. The caller holds the controller lock
# across every call; nothing in this file takes one.
#
# xhci_pci.c is the other side of that split and the only file that touches
# BAR0 or PCI config space; xhci_init.c is the controller start-up sequence
# written against it, xhci_evt.c is the interrupt path (ISR body and event-
# ring drain), xhci_cmd.c is the asynchronous command engine - the one file
# that holds the miniport's own interior spin lock - xhci_rh.c is the root-hub
# callback family, which reads and writes PORTSC under that lock, and
# xhci_slot.c is the Phase 6 device layer: slots, EP0, and the command chain
# that addresses a device. None of the six belongs in the pure list above.
#
# xhci_log.c is task 11-V.7's optional log ring, and it is pure on the same
# terms: a bounded byte ring in the miniport extension with its wrap accounting
# and its flush *decision*, and no file I/O of any kind. The things that need
# the DDK - the two registry switches, the IRQL measurement that refuses a flush
# above PASSIVE_LEVEL, and the DbgPrint emission - are in xhci_dispatch.c.
# (This comment also named ZwCreateFile/ZwWriteFile/ZwClose until task 13-L.2
# retired the ring-0 file sink; all three imports left the binary with it, so
# the driver now performs no file I/O anywhere.) Unlike xhci_dbg.c
# this one is compiled into the **release** build too, deliberately: shipping
# the debug binary as the diagnostic build would reproduce the
# DbgPrint-from-DPC bugcheck the task exists to route around (roadmap task
# 12.2).
#
# xhci_dbg.c is the QEMU-flavour trace channel; every function in it compiles
# away without XHCI_DBG_LIVE, which this file defines for chk_qemu and for
# nothing else, so neither published binary carries any of its imports. (This
# comment said "debug-build" until the post-Phase 13 review rounds corrected
# it, from before the split below.)
#
# xhci98.rc is task 8-A.4's file version resource. It is listed in SOURCES like
# any other input - the DDK's build engine runs rc.exe on a .rc entry - and it
# adds **no import**, because a resource is data in its own .rsrc section rather
# than code. It also does not disturb LINKER_FLAGS below, which merges .rdata
# and says nothing about .rsrc. Both claims are re-checked by the post-link
# import gate rather than trusted: build with scripts\build-driver.cmd.
#
# xhci_probe.c is task 6-V.1's transfer-contract probe. It is instrumentation
# rather than behaviour - nothing in the driver branches on anything it
# produces - but it is *not* in the pure list and must not be moved there: it
# takes the controller lock, so it depends on xhci_hw.h. Its classification
# compiles into the release build too, deliberately: the counters are the
# only reading of the contract a release binary can give.
#
SOURCES=xhci_mem.c    \
        xhci_ring.c   \
        xhci_caps.c   \
        xhci_port.c   \
        xhci_ctx.c    \
        xhci_xfer.c   \
        xhci_topo.c   \
        xhci_desc.c   \
        xhci_log.c    \
        xhci_dbg.c    \
        xhci_pci.c    \
        xhci_init.c   \
        xhci_evt.c    \
        xhci_cmd.c    \
        xhci_rh.c     \
        xhci_slot.c   \
        xhci_probe.c  \
        xhci_dispatch.c \
        xhci98.rc

#
# USBPORT_RegisterUSBPortDriver and USBPORT_GetHciMn are private exports of
# usbport.sys and the Win2K DDK ships no import library for them. This one is
# generated from __stdcall stubs, not from `lib /def:` - see
# docs\contributing\build-and-test.md "Build Files" for why that distinction matters.
#
TARGETLIBS=.\usbport.lib

#
# Diagnostic-only escape hatch, empty in every normal build. Set it in the
# environment before building to pass extra -D flags through, e.g.
#   set XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES=-DXHCI_PROBE_RESOURCES_SIZE=4096
#   set XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES=-DXHCI_FAIL_START_CONTROLLER
# Nothing in the tree sets it.
#
# The second one is roadmap task 12.3's failed-start artifact: the init sequence
# refuses its last step, so the driver installs, loads, and then fails inside
# StartController - which is the one recovery route neither target has ever
# exercised. It is the only diagnostic build that may be *packaged*, and only
# through make-package.ps1 -FailStartArtifact, which requires the second marker
# xhci_dispatch.c emits for it. The rule below is unchanged and is what makes
# that exception safe to have: every diagnostic build still carries the
# do-not-deploy marker as well.
#
# Any nonempty value also defines XHCI_DIAGNOSTIC_BUILD, which is what embeds
# the marker make-package.ps1 refuses to package. That generalisation is repo
# audit's finding 4: the marker used to hang off XHCI_PROBE_RESOURCES_
# SIZE alone, so a binary built with any *other* extra define was warned about
# at build time and then accepted by the packager - a warning is not a gate, and
# the one artifact that must never ship was the one relying on it. It is set
# here rather than in build-driver.cmd so it also holds for a bare `build` from
# a DDK prompt, which is the invocation that gets no warning at all.
#
#
# Task 12.3's artifact must be the whole of XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES, not part of it.
# Review round 2 finding 4: every build carrying XHCI_FAIL_START_CONTROLLER
# emits XHCI98_FAILSTART_ARTIFACT_TASK_12_3, and make-package.ps1
# -FailStartArtifact keys its one exception to the do-not-deploy rule on that
# string - so `-DXHCI_FAIL_START_CONTROLLER -DANYTHING_ELSE` is staged as the
# artifact while behaving like neither artifact. xhci_dispatch.c can only
# refuse the one other define this tree names, because C cannot ask what else
# was defined; the exact test belongs here, where the value is a string.
#
# It is in this file rather than in build-driver.cmd for the same reason
# XHCI_DIAGNOSTIC_BUILD is: a bare `build` from a DDK prompt reads `sources`
# and never sees the wrapper's gates. The findstr runs at parse time and its
# exit code is 0 when the substring is present.
#
!IF "$(XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES)" != ""
!IF [echo $(XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES)| findstr /c:"XHCI_FAIL_START_CONTROLLER" >nul]==0
!IF "$(XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES)" != "-DXHCI_FAIL_START_CONTROLLER"
!ERROR XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES names XHCI_FAIL_START_CONTROLLER alongside something else. Task 12.3's failed-start artifact must be built alone - set XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES to exactly -DXHCI_FAIL_START_CONTROLLER - because any build carrying that define is staged as the artifact by make-package.ps1 -FailStartArtifact.
!ENDIF
!ENDIF
C_DEFINES=$(C_DEFINES) $(XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES) -DXHCI_DIAGNOSTIC_BUILD
!ELSE
C_DEFINES=$(C_DEFINES)
!ENDIF

#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The three build flavours (roadmap task 13-L.1)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# BUILD_ALT_DIR is the DDK's own flavour discriminator: build.exe concatenates
# it onto `obj` to make the output tree, and setenv.bat sets it to fre or chk.
# scripts\build-driver.cmd overrides it to chk_qemu for the third flavour -
# debug and qemu are BOTH checked builds, so without a distinct tree they
# overwrite each other's objects in src\objchk\i386. Verified against this
# DDK's build.exe: the value may be at most 10 characters, the
# obj tree becomes src\objchk_qemu\i386 and the log becomes buildchk_qemu.log,
# and the lib paths still resolve because setenv.bat expands them eagerly.
#
#   release  BUILD_ALT_DIR=fre       free     ships, default
#   debug    BUILD_ALT_DIR=chk       checked  ships, the diagnostic download
#   qemu     BUILD_ALT_DIR=chk_qemu  checked  never published
#
# The flavour is decided here rather than by a variable of this project's own,
# so that a bare `build` from a DDK prompt cannot produce a binary whose defines
# and whose obj tree disagree - the same reason XHCI_DIAGNOSTIC_BUILD is set in
# this file and not in the wrapper.
#
# TWO defines make the qemu flavour, and they are two because the hazards they
# carry are two (src/xhci_dbg.h has the long form):
#
#   XHCI_DBG_LIVE  the live per-line trace itself. A *runtime* hazard: that
#                  profile, from DPC and ISR contexts at real interrupt rates,
#                  is what bugchecks Windows 98 on metal across three device
#                  classes. Without it src/xhci_dbg.c compiles away entirely.
#   XHCI_DBG_E9    the port-0xE9 mirror inside it. A *load-time* hazard: it
#                  needs HAL.dll!WRITE_PORT_UCHAR, the sole import delta
#                  between 0.0.0.4's two published binaries, of which the debug
#                  one gives the E460 a Code 2 and loads nothing at all. WHY
#                  that build failed is NOT established - defect 2b,
#                  docs/contributing/runs/run-13e.md P6.
#
# **Both are POSITIVE and opt-IN, and that polarity is the repair rather than a
# tidy-up** (design record 08 section 5.1): the safe configuration is the
# default and the emulator convenience is the thing asked for by name. The
# shipping `debug` flavour carries neither, which is what its defining
# requirement - that it LOADS AND RUNS on real Windows 98 and Windows 2000
# metal - actually costs. What `debug` is for is the log ring, which is
# recorded from any IRQL and handed over in one bulk dump from the PASSIVE
# flush: recording is not emission.
#
# They reach the compiler WITHOUT XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES, which is what makes the
# qemu flavour packageable-in-principle rather than marked undeployable;
# make-package.ps1 refuses to publish it by flavour instead.
#
# XHCI_FLAVOUR_* is the in-image flavour marker. VS_FF_DEBUG cannot separate
# debug from qemu - both are checked builds - and a user sending a capture has
# to be able to say which binary produced it.
#
!IF "$(BUILD_ALT_DIR)" == "chk_qemu"
C_DEFINES=$(C_DEFINES) -DXHCI_DBG_LIVE -DXHCI_DBG_E9 -DXHCI_FLAVOUR_QEMU
!ELSEIF "$(BUILD_ALT_DIR)" == "chk"
C_DEFINES=$(C_DEFINES) -DXHCI_FLAVOUR_DEBUG
!ELSEIF "$(BUILD_ALT_DIR)" == "fre"
C_DEFINES=$(C_DEFINES) -DXHCI_FLAVOUR_RELEASE
!ELSE
!ERROR BUILD_ALT_DIR is "$(BUILD_ALT_DIR)" and this tree knows exactly three flavours - fre (release), chk (debug) and chk_qemu (qemu). setenv.bat sets the first two; scripts\build-driver.cmd sets the third. A build with any other value would carry no flavour marker and no decision about the port-0xE9 mirror, so it is refused here rather than shipped as an unidentifiable binary.
!ENDIF

#
# Matches the shipping NUSB/SP4 miniports: no separate .rdata section.
#
LINKER_FLAGS=-merge:.rdata=.text
